  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-MNG826</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The commitments under ratified ILO conventions are fulfilled  and additional conventions ratified using effective consultation with social partners and other stakeholders</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mongolia continues to face challenges in fully upholding fundamental principles and rights at work because child labour enforcement, discrimination, and the high prevalence of informal employment remain persistent gaps, limiting equal protection and leaving many workers outside legal and institutional safeguards; at the same time, labour standards violations continue in sectors such as construction, services, and transportation, and trade unions require stronger systems and modern tools to effectively reach workers, deliver services, and advocate for improved working conditions&#x2014;factors that collectively hinder consistent, practical application of international labour standards in line with ILO conventions.
          The overall situation is steadily improving, supported by a strong legal environment&#x2014;including the Labour Law adopted in 2021&#x2014;and Mongolia&#x2019;s commitment to international standards. The country has ratified 21 ILO Conventions, including nine of the ten fundamental Conventions, and submits regular reports to the ILO. The Government has also expressed its intention to ratify Conventions 81, 129, 187, and 190, further strengthening alignment with international labour norms. At the national level, the Tripartite Committee on Labour and Social Partnership is developing the next three-year social and labour priorities for 2026, which will guide future cooperation with the ILO.
          Expected results: 1) Mongolia ratifies additional fundamental, governance and technical conventions; 2) Increased capacity of tripartite constituents to drive the effective application of international labour standards, demonstrated by their meaningful participation in national processes to identify and address gaps between national law and practice and the requirements of targeted Conventions; 3) ILO sectoral Code of Conducts are introduced and effectively applied.</narrative>
  </description>
